 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Hermite Data Computation via Bisection](822-hermite-data-computation-via-bisection.md)
-Hermite Data Computation via Bisection is a method for precisely locating isosurface intersections on the edges of an adaptive octree's leaf cells and computing associated surface normals. It solves the problem of geometric inaccuracy in dual contouring, where linear interpolation between corner values introduces visible jaggedness on curved or sharp features. Unlike [Octree Path Computation](897-octree-path-computation.md) which focuses on traversal efficiency, or [Sparse Voxel Octree Ray Traversal](2451-sparse-voxel-octree-ray-traversal.md) which samples volume density, this method refines intersection positions to sub-cell accuracy to enable accurate Quadratic Error Function (QEF) vertex placement.
-
-### [Octree Path Computation](897-octree-path-computation.md)
-Octree Path Computation encodes a node's spatial location within an octree hierarchy into a compact bit-vector (path) based on traversal coordinates. It identifies the child node a ray enters at each depth level without repeated geometric intersection tests against child bounding boxes. It differs from [Hermite Data Computation via Bisection](822-hermite-data-computation-via-bisection.md) by using direct coordinate comparison rather than iterative refinement, and from [Rational Ray Parameterization for Octree Traversal](3875-rational-ray-parameterization-for-octree-traversal.md) by operating in grid-space rather than ray-parameter space [G].
-
-### [Rational Ray Parameterization for Octree Traversal](3875-rational-ray-parameterization-for-octree-traversal.md)
-Rational Ray Parameterization for Octree Traversal is a method for tracing rays through an octree using exact rational arithmetic to avoid floating-point precision errors during cell boundary crossing and neighbor finding. It solves the problem of determining the next cell intersected by a ray when the ray parameters are not integers, which typically occurs when the viewplane is in an arbitrary position in space. Unlike sibling methods that rely on floating-point comparisons or pre-computed structures, this method guarantees robustness by representing ray parameters as rational numbers (numerator/denominator pairs) and using integer arithmetic for all comparisons and boundary tests.
+Hermite Data Computation via Bisection precisely locates isosurface intersections on the edges of an adaptive octree's leaf cells and computes surface normals. It solves geometric inaccuracy in dual contouring by refining intersection positions to sub-cell accuracy for Quadratic Error Function (QEF) vertex placement, unlike [Octree Path Computation](897-octree-path-computation.md) which focuses on traversal efficiency.
 
 ### [Stackless Traversal](5202-stackless-traversal.md)
-Stackless Traversal is a ray-traversal algorithm for hierarchical volume structures (such as N3-trees or octrees) that eliminates the need for an explicit stack data structure by recalculating Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) parameters at every descent and ascent step. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [DDA Tree Traversal with Mini-Stack](5244-dda-tree-traversal-with-mini-stack.md) by trading increased computational cost for reduced memory footprint, making it particularly suitable for hardware or GPU architectures where stack storage is limited or expensive.
+Stackless Traversal eliminates the explicit stack data structure in hierarchical volume traversal by recalculating Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) parameters at every descent and ascent step. It trades increased computational cost for zero stack memory usage, making it advantageous in memory-constrained environments like early GPU architectures.
 
 ### [GPU Octree Volume Rasterization with Shadow Mapping](5209-gpu-octree-volume-rasterization-with-shadow-mapping.md)
-This method implements high-performance GPU volume rendering by decoupling the branch-intensive octree traversal from the raycasting loop, using a rasterization pass to identify active volume regions and a shadow mapping pass for lighting. It solves the performance bottleneck of traditional raycasting on sparse data by moving empty-space culling into a graphics pipeline stage, achieving 2 to 4 times faster rendering speeds than state-of-the-art algorithms on large out-of-core datasets. Unlike sibling methods that rely on pure ray-tracing traversal or fixed-step DDA, this approach uses view-dependent proxy geometry rasterization to generate a per-pixel list of penetrated bricks, ensuring front-to-back processing and tighter ray segments. It builds on [Octree and Tree Traversal](4134-octree-and-tree-traversal.md) and relates to [Stackless Traversal](5202-stackless-traversal.md) and [DDA Tree Traversal with Mini-Stack](5244-dda-tree-traversal-with-mini-stack.md).
+This method decouples branch-intensive octree traversal from the raycasting loop using a rasterization pass to identify active volume regions and a shadow mapping pass for lighting. It solves performance bottlenecks on sparse data by moving empty-space culling into a graphics pipeline stage, achieving 2 to 4 times faster rendering than state-of-the-art algorithms on large out-of-core datasets.
 
 ### [DDA Tree Traversal with Mini-Stack](5244-dda-tree-traversal-with-mini-stack.md)
-This method extends the Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) algorithm for regular grids to N-ary trees (specifically octrees) on GPU shaders using a small, fixed-size stack implemented via dynamically indexed local arrays. It solves the problem of efficiently traversing sparse hierarchical structures by combining the branch-free, ordered traversal of DDA with explicit stack management for backtracking, avoiding the overhead of recursion or large dynamic heaps. It is distinct from [Stackless Traversal](5202-stackless-traversal.md) by using an explicit mini-stack, and from [Octree Path Computation](897-octree-path-computation.md) by relying on DDA steps within grid levels rather than path-based indexing.
-
-### [Octree with Neighbor Pointers](5699-octree-with-neighbor-pointers.md)
-This method augments a branch-on-need octree with explicit links to adjacent nodes to accelerate ray traversal in volume rendering. By allowing direct jumps to neighboring nodes across face boundaries, it eliminates the need for backtracking up the tree hierarchy when a ray exits a subtree. It differs from full adjacency structures (e.g., Zhou et al. [2010]) by maintaining only x-neighbor pointers (links to the 6 face-adjacent nodes) rather than all 26 neighbors, and is typically constructed on-the-fly per frame from a view-dependent octree.
-
-### [Presence Acceleration](5753-presence-acceleration.md)
-Presence Acceleration is a ray traversal technique that uses a binary pyramid to skip empty regions in a volume by identifying cells containing at least one opaque voxel. It solves the problem of inefficiently traversing large transparent areas in volume data. Unlike pointer-based octrees, it relies on a fixed-resolution, pointerless grid hierarchy that allows for simple, predictable memory access patterns.
+This method extends the Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) algorithm to N-ary hierarchical trees for GPU shader implementation. It solves efficient traversal of sparse voxel structures by maintaining a small, fixed-size stack of DDA states, allowing descent and ascent without dynamic memory allocation.
 
 ### [Compositing Tree Rendering](5860-compositing-tree-rendering.md)
-Compositing Tree Rendering is a time-varying volume rendering strategy that minimizes computational cost by rendering only modified data subtrees and compositing them with retained partial images of unmodified data. It differs from standard incremental rendering by maintaining a persistent hierarchical structure (the compositing tree) that mirrors the data's compressed octree, allowing for efficient spatial updates without re-rendering the entire volume for every time step.
-
-### [Bounding-Sphere Tree Traversal with Frame-Rate Control](7227-bounding-sphere-tree-traversal-with-frame-rate-control.md)
-This method renders hierarchical tree structures (specifically Shell and Solid trees) using point-based splatting primitives, solving the problem of maintaining real-time frame rates for large volumetric datasets by dynamically adjusting rendering fidelity. It distinguishes itself from standard octree traversal by replacing fixed-resolution voxel stepping with adaptive bounding-sphere culling and frame-rate feedback loops that control the minimum splat size in screen space.
+Compositing Tree Rendering minimizes computational cost for time-varying volume rendering by rendering only modified data subtrees and compositing them with retained partial images of unmodified data. It differs from standard incremental rendering by maintaining a persistent hierarchical structure that mirrors the data's compressed octree, enabling efficient spatial updates without full re-rendering.
 
 ### [Tree Marcher](7534-tree-marcher.md)
-The Tree Marcher is a hierarchical ray traversal algorithm for generic grid structures. It solves the problem of efficiently sampling sparse volumetric data by combining hierarchical empty-space skipping with Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) stepping within occupied regions. It explicitly manages the transition between tree-level traversal and voxel-level stepping using a fixed-step DDA, handling arbitrary grid topologies defined by template parameters.
+The Tree Marcher is a hierarchical ray traversal algorithm designed to step a ray through a generic grid structure (such as OpenVDB) to identify intersections with active nodes. It solves efficient navigation of sparse, multi-resolution volumetric data by skipping empty subtrees and returning precise intersection intervals for occupied nodes, bridging coarse hierarchical culling and fine-grained voxel sampling.
+
+### [Camera-Driven Octree Subdivision](7636-camera-driven-octree-subdivision.md)
+Camera-Driven Octree Subdivision constructs a spatial hierarchy by recursively splitting nodes based on the proximity of visible camera centers to the node's center. It solves efficient mapping of 3D space into local warp spaces for multi-view rendering by ensuring regions near cameras are represented with higher resolution, using camera-centric distance metrics rather than data density.
 
 ### [JIT Type Specialization](7740-jit-type-specialization.md)
-JIT Type Specialization is a technique where type resolution and function routing are performed at Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation time, inlining the traversal, type check, and address lookup into the final function to eliminate switch statements and runtime type resolving overhead. It sets itself apart from standard dynamic dispatch by making access time independent of the number of different elemental node types, as the specialized code paths are baked directly into the executable.
+JIT Type Specialization performs type resolution and function routing at Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation time, inlining traversal, type checks, and address lookups to eliminate switch statements and runtime overhead. It sets itself apart from standard dynamic dispatch by making access time independent of the number of elemental node types, as specialized code paths are baked into the executable.
 
 ### [Ray Traversal Algorithms](7918-ray-traversal-algorithms.md)
-Ray Traversal Algorithms are hierarchical intersection methods that determine which nodes of an octree (or similar spatial structure) a ray intersects, enabling efficient rendering of sparse voxel data by skipping empty space. Unlike uniform grid ray marching which samples every voxel, these methods use the tree hierarchy to jump over empty regions, reducing computational cost. They differ from BVH-based approaches by operating directly on the octree structure without an intermediate bounding volume hierarchy, although some implementations may convert the SVO to a BVH for specific traversal optimizations.
-
-### [Frustum Culling Integration](8798-frustum-culling-integration.md)
-Frustum Culling Integration is a technique used in progressive volume rendering (specifically for Gaussian Splatting) to prioritize the loading and rendering of splats visible to the user by applying frustum culling. It is applied after global ordering is determined to refine the selection of data based on the current viewpoint. It sets itself apart from the base octree-only approach by leveraging user-specific view information to improve foreground quality, whereas the octree-only method provides a good scene representation without requiring such knowledge.
+Ray Traversal Algorithms are a family of hierarchical methods that determine the sequence of nodes or cells intersected by a ray within a spatial data structure, primarily to accelerate volume rendering and surface intersection tests. This family is distinguished from [Stackless Traversal](5202-stackless-traversal.md) by its explicit reliance on parent-child pointer relationships and backtracking logic rather than encoding traversal state into a bitstream.
 
 ### [Tetrahedralization of Octree Cells](9049-tetrahedralization-of-octree-cells.md)
-Tetrahedralization of Octree Cells is a technique for generating a manifold isosurface from an adaptive octree by constructing a dual simplicial partition. It solves the problem of self-intersections and non-manifold vertices inherent in adaptive octrees by ensuring convexity and manifold topology through a specific dual structure. Unlike Dual Marching Tetrahedra (DMC), which can produce self-intersecting "flaps" because it does not strictly respect the primal octree grid's convexity, this method guarantees a manifold surface. Compared to Dual Contouring (DC), it samples the function at a higher effective resolution by utilizing extra samples on edges, faces, and cells, though at the cost of increased computation time.
+Tetrahedralization of Octree Cells generates a manifold isosurface from an adaptive octree by constructing a dual simplicial partition. It solves self-intersections and non-manifold vertices inherent in adaptive octrees by creating a dual structure that ensures convexity and manifold topology, unlike Dual Marching Tetrahedra (DMC) which can produce self-intersecting flaps.
 
 ### [Topology-Caching for Dynamic Particle Volume Raycasting](9341-topology-caching-for-dynamic-particle-volume-raycasting.md)
-Topology-Caching is a GPU-based volume rendering technique for the interactive visualization of dynamic particle systems (e.g., SPH) that reconstructs a scalar field via raycasting without preprocessing. It solves the performance bottleneck of gathering particle contributions for field reconstruction by using three GPU-managed caches: a node-cache for accelerated tree traversal, an influence-cache for fast particle access, and a slab-cache for intermediate compositing. Unlike scattering approaches that cause memory collisions or splatting techniques that require sorting, this method uses a gathering principle with coherent ray packets and stackless traversal to achieve interactive frame rates for hundreds of thousands of particles.
+Topology-Caching is a GPU-based volume rendering technique for interactive visualization of dynamic particle systems (e.g., SPH) that reconstructs a scalar field via raycasting without preprocessing. It solves the performance bottleneck of gathering particle contributions by using three GPU-managed caches: a node-cache for accelerated tree traversal, an influence-cache for fast particle access, and a slab-cache for intermediate compositing.
 
 ### [Budgeted-GS Factoring LOD](9677-budgeted-gs-factoring-lod.md)
-Budgeted-GS Factoring LOD is a hierarchical level-of-detail (LOD) method for real-time large-scale Gaussian Splatting that reduces rendering cost by aggregating primitives into equivalent planks based on a pixel-footprint budget. It solves the problem of rendering millions of Gaussians in real-time by dynamically selecting between raw primitives, fine equivalent planks, or coarse aggregate planks depending on their distance and scale relative to the camera. Unlike standard compression methods that operate in an over-budgeted regime with flat error curves, this method achieves a steeper error reduction (slope approaching -0.5) by using a "floor law" to select the optimal budget, allowing for significant size reduction (119× smaller than input) while maintaining visual fidelity.
+Budgeted-GS Factoring LOD is a hierarchical level-of-detail method for real-time large-scale Gaussian Splatting that reduces rendering cost by aggregating primitives into equivalent planks based on a pixel-footprint budget. It solves rendering millions of Gaussians in real-time by dynamically selecting between raw primitives, fine equivalent planks, or coarse aggregate planks depending on distance and scale relative to the camera.
 
-### [Octree Raycasting](11317-octree-raycasting.md)
-Octree Raycasting is an algorithm to find all octants in a pointer-based octree that intersect with a given ray, returning them sorted by distance (closest first). It uses parametric ray representation ($t$-values) to traverse the octree structure efficiently. It differs from [Octree Path Computation](897-octree-path-computation.md) by explicitly computing and sorting intersection intervals for each visited octant rather than just determining path connectivity, and from [Stackless Traversal](5202-stackless-traversal.md) by relying on a specific recursive descent logic with explicit $t$-value bounds passed down the call stack.
+### [Hierarchical Tree Node Traversal](12213-hierarchical-tree-node-traversal.md)
+Hierarchical Tree Node Traversal accelerates ray-volume intersection by traversing a sparse octree structure to identify contiguous segments of active data. It solves inefficiently sampling empty space in large volumetric datasets by skipping inactive nodes and resolving hits at the node level rather than the individual voxel level, providing entry and exit times for line-integration.
+
+### [Frustum and Visibility Culling](12355-frustum-and-visibility-culling.md)
+Frustum and Visibility Culling methods optimize volume rendering by identifying and discarding geometry that does not contribute to the final image, thereby reducing computational load. This family distinguishes itself from sibling traversal methods by decoupling visibility determination from the core sampling loop, often serving as a pre-pass or integrated filter to limit the working set of voxels, splats, or bricks.
+
+### [Data Structure and Indexing](12420-data-structure-and-indexing.md)
+Data Structure and Indexing defines how hierarchical volume data (typically octrees) is organized in memory and accessed during ray traversal. It solves efficiently mapping spatial coordinates to volume samples while minimizing memory bandwidth and traversal overhead, focusing on storage layout and connectivity required to support traversal algorithms.
+
+### [Octree Sampled SDF Dataset](13111-octree-sampled-sdf-dataset.md)
+An Octree Sampled SDF Dataset generates signed-distance function (SDF) samples from a sparse octree acceleration structure constructed from a single mesh shape. It limits sampling to occupied octree cells, supports equal distribution of samples per cell, and allows online resampling via a cached narrowband pool, optionally including RGB data derived from mesh textures.
+
+### [Spatial Hierarchies](13154-spatial-hierarchies.md)
+Spatial hierarchies organize volumetric or geometric primitives into a tree structure to accelerate ray intersection tests and point location. They solve the problem of $O(N)$ linear scans by enabling $O(\log N)$ traversal, reducing the number of samples required to render a volume, and adapt to data sparsity by skipping empty regions entirely.
+
+### [Octree-based Entropy Rendering](13580-octree-based-entropy-rendering.md)
+Octree-based Entropy Rendering accelerates volume rendering by using hierarchical error metrics stored in non-leaf octree nodes to skip or approximate low-entropy regions. Unlike standard traversal which visits all occupied nodes, this method dynamically adjusts traversal granularity based on a user-defined error tolerance or time budget, trading visual fidelity for performance in smooth or transparent areas.
 
 ### Photon Splatting for Direct Illumination
-- **Definition:** Store incoming radiance from a light source in the octree leaves by rendering the scene from the light's view using standard rasterization to output a world position map (light-view map), where each pixel represents a photon [S1].
-- **Steps:**
-    - Splat photons using a fragment shader with one thread per light-view-map pixel, storing them as a direction distribution and energy proportional to the subtended solid angle of the pixel as seen from the light [S1].
-    - Assume light-view map resolution is higher than the lowest level of the voxel grid to splat photons directly into leaf nodes without gaps [S1].
-    - Store photons at the finest level of the voxel structure because they are stored at the surface [S1].
-    - Use atomic add to handle multiple photons ending up in the same voxel [S1].
-- **Pitfalls:**
-    - Atomic add operations are currently only available for integer textures. Use a 16-bit normalized texture format, denormalized when accessing the value later [S1].
-    - Voxels are repeated for adjacent bricks for fast hardware-supported filtering. Copying photons directly to all required locations in adjacent bricks leads to many thread collisions and bandwidth issues [S1].
+Store incoming radiance from a light source in the octree leaves by rendering the scene from the light's view using standard rasterization to output a world position map (light-view map), where each pixel represents a photon [S1]. Photons are splatted using a fragment shader with one thread per light-view-map pixel, storing them as a direction distribution and energy proportional to the subtended solid angle of the pixel as seen from the light [S1]. Atomic add operations are used to handle multiple photons ending up in the same voxel, though they are currently only available for integer textures, requiring a 16-bit normalized texture format denormalized upon access [S1]. Voxels are repeated for adjacent bricks for fast hardware-supported filtering, but copying photons directly to all required locations in adjacent bricks leads to many thread collisions and bandwidth issues [S1].
 
